4. African Imperialism: The History Before European Colonial Rule

A serious comparison must also examine African imperialism.

Africa was not a continent without states, political competition, or empire before European colonization.

Across different regions and historical periods, African societies developed kingdoms, city-states, federations, and empires. Some expanded through commerce and diplomacy; others relied heavily on military conquest, tribute, forced labor, or slavery.

The term African imperialism therefore describes a range of historical experiences rather than one continent-wide project.

A. The Mali Empire

The Mali Empire expanded across parts of West Africa through political consolidation, military power, and control of important commercial routes.

Its rulers benefited from trans-Saharan trade in gold and other commodities.

Under Mansa Musa, Mali became famous for its wealth and connections to the wider Islamic world. Its urban centers participated in commercial and intellectual networks that extended far beyond West Africa.

However, Mali’s expansion also involved political subordination and the collection of revenue from territories under its authority.

Like other empires, its power depended partly on its ability to maintain relationships with provincial authorities and subordinate communities.

B. The Songhai Empire

The Songhai Empire provides an especially useful comparison.

Songhai expanded under rulers such as Sunni Ali, who used military force to conquer territories and control important cities, including Timbuktu and Djenné.

Its government benefited from agriculture, taxation, and trans-Saharan commerce.

Songhai’s imperial system demonstrates that territorial expansion, taxation, military organization, and the subordination of neighboring communities were not exclusively European practices. 

C. The Ethiopian Empire

The history of the Ethiopian Empire further illustrates the complexity of African imperialism.

During the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, Ethiopian rulers expanded imperial control over territories inhabited by diverse populations.

Research on the incorporation of Bela-Shangul and Gumuz documents how imperial authorities and local power holders participated in systems of tribute and slave trading, despite Ethiopia’s public commitments to oppose slavery. 

This history complicates any simple division between an entirely imperialist Europe and an entirely anti-imperialist Africa.

African rulers, like rulers elsewhere, sometimes built states through conquest and extraction.

At the same time, African societies also resisted external domination, including European colonialism.

Recognizing African empires does not justify European colonialism. It places African political history within the wider history of states, power, and empire.

