8. What Would an African Strategy for Sovereignty Look Like?

The historical comparison suggests that African governments need not choose between Chinese influence and European influence as though these were the only possible paths to development.

A more comprehensive strategy would focus on building African economic and political capacity.

A. Diversify international partnerships

African governments can negotiate with Chinese, European, American, Indian, Japanese, and other partners.

Competition among investors can improve bargaining positions, provided governments have the expertise and institutional capacity to evaluate competing offers.

B. Build domestic industrial capacity

Exporting raw materials can generate revenue, but countries may capture more value by developing processing, manufacturing, engineering, and related services.

For example, mineral-producing countries can evaluate whether local refining and processing are economically viable rather than relying exclusively on exporting unprocessed materials.

C. Strengthen contract transparency

Public disclosure of major infrastructure, mining, and financing agreements can help citizens understand the distribution of costs, benefits, and risks.

Independent audits and competitive procurement can reduce opportunities for corruption.

D. Invest in education and technical expertise

Governments need engineers, economists, lawyers, geologists, accountants, and public administrators capable of negotiating complex international agreements.

Domestic expertise can reduce dependence on external advisers and strengthen the ability to enforce contracts.

E. Strengthen regional cooperation

African states can coordinate infrastructure development, trade policy, industrial strategy, and investment negotiations through regional institutions and the African Continental Free Trade Area.

Larger integrated markets may create opportunities that individual national economies cannot easily achieve alone.

F. Evaluate partnerships through outcomes

Governments should measure whether international partnerships improve productivity, employment, public revenue, living standards, and technological capacity.

Diplomatic symbolism is not a substitute for economic results.

These principles are not inherently anti-Chinese or anti-European. They are strategies for strengthening African sovereignty regardless of the external partner involved.

