9. The Larger Historical Lesson: Empire Is About Power, Not Geography

The story of Zheng He allows us to move beyond a simplistic division between peaceful Eastern civilizations and aggressive Western civilizations—or the reverse.

The Ming dynasty projected imperial power through maritime expeditions and a hierarchical diplomatic system.

European powers developed commercial and military networks that, in many African territories, evolved into direct colonial rule.

African empires also exercised power through conquest, taxation, tribute, and political incorporation, while other African societies developed different forms of government and resisted imperial expansion.

These histories were not identical.

The most important distinctions concern the institutions of power: whether rulers demanded tribute, controlled trade, conquered territory, imposed racial hierarchies, appropriated land, extracted labor, or established permanent administrations over foreign populations.

The consequences of those actions also differed across regions and historical periods.

For Kenya, Zheng He’s voyages illustrate an era when African coastal societies were connected to the wider world through trade and diplomacy, centuries before British colonial rule.

For contemporary Africa, the comparison offers a practical lesson: the essential question is not whether an international partner is Chinese, European, American, or African. It is whether the relationship strengthens the sovereignty, prosperity, and freedom of the people involved.

That is the standard by which historical empires—and modern international partnerships—should be examined.

